  "chapters": [
    {
      "chapterId": "cap-inv",
      "slug": "invariant-as-support",
      "title": "Invariant as support",
      "order": 1,
      "sections": [
        {
          "sectionId": "sec-que-es-invariante",
          "slug": "definition-and-use",
          "title": "Definition and use",
          "order": 1,
          "kind": "theory",
          "trackProgress": true,
          "estimatedMinutes": 18,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "blk-m6-s1-p1",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A loop invariant is a property that holds each time the guard is about to be evaluated again. In formal courses you prove initialization, maintenance, and termination. Here we use it to "
                },
                {
                  "type": "strong",
                  "text": "reason about correctness"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", not to obtain complexity order by itself."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-m6-s1-algo",
              "type": "algorithm",
              "language": "pseudocode",
              "title": "Example: sum 1..n with WHILE",
              "code": "sum_to_n(n) BEGIN\n  s <- 0;\n  i <- 1;\n  WHILE i <= n DO BEGIN\n    s <- s + i;\n    i <- i + 1;\n  END\n  RETURN s;\nEND",
              "caption": "Typical invariant (at WHILE entry): s is the sum of 1..i-1. At exit, i = n+1 and s is the sum of 1..n."
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-m6-s1-tool",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Open a similar program in the "
                },
                {
                  "type": "link",
                  "text": "analyzer",
                  "target": { "kind": "external", "ref": "/analyzer" }
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " and, if the UI shows an invariant suggestion, compare it to what you would write: it should line up with initialization, maintenance, and loop exit."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-m6-s1-ev",
              "type": "evidenceBlock",
              "variant": "concept",
              "icon": "school",
              "title": "Suggestion in the tool",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "id": "blk-m6-s1-ev-p",
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "If the product offers an invariant suggestion, use it as a draft for your own argument. Check edge cases and counterexamples: the suggestion does not replace a proof or guarantee the most efficient loop."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "sectionId": "sec-no-prueba",
          "slug": "not-a-formal-proof",
          "title": "Not a formal proof",
          "order": 2,
          "kind": "theory",
          "trackProgress": true,
          "estimatedMinutes": 12,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "blk-m6-s2-p1",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Complexity and correctness answer different questions. A poorly chosen invariant can be vague or false; the tool does not replace instructor judgment or written proof. Keep it modest: "
                },
                {
                  "type": "strong",
                  "text": "pedagogical support"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", not automatic certification."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-m6-s2-ex",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Example: claiming “the array is sorted at the end” may hold by luck on one test; a useful invariant relates each iteration’s state to the goal (e.g. “the first i entries are the i smallest”)."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
